  {
    "name": "t06_meet_the_machine",
    "trailer": "6",
    "light": "overcast",
    "_why": "TRAILER 11.1, and this is the second version of it. It was t06_bench_machine: four seconds of a machine on a trestle inside the service bay, dark, half the body behind a bench leg. The card at 0:46 then says you cannot drive IT and the pronoun has nothing to point at. The FIRST attempt at a replacement kept it on the hoist cradle, in daylight, whole body, head turning -- and it still read as equipment, because a thing standing on a table is a thing being worked on. So it came off the table. THE CAMERA IS AT MACHINE HEIGHT, 0.45 m, level with its head: you have to crouch to meet it, which is the difference between looking at a tool and looking at an animal. 1.7 m away on a 50 mm, so the body fills three-fifths of the frame and the feet are in it. FIVE SECONDS, the longest the treatment allows, and 0.39 m of push -- the slowest move in the cut, 0.15 m/s at its peak. AND ITS HEAD MOVES: it starts turned away and down at its own flank, where the work is, and comes round to the lens over the last two seconds. That turn is the shot. T4 rather than T2.8 because at 1.7 m a 50 mm wide open is 11 cm deep and the sonar bar would be sharp with the eye already soft.",
